// Lexical scoping, both answers in one body.
//
// `kept` is allocated before the loop and read after it, so its uses span blocks
// and no loop encloses them: it keeps the frame's arena and dies at return. The
// loop's own site never escapes the loop, so it gets an arena of its own that is
// created on the loop's entry edge and destroyed on its exit edge. Two scopes,
// two regions, two create/destroy pairs.
int function main() {
    var total = 0;
    var index = 0;
    var count = 8192;
    var kept = memory.allocate<int>(count);
    kept[0] = 7;
    while (index < 3) {
        var value = memory.allocate<int>(count);
        value[0] = index;
        total = total + value[0];
        index = index + 1;
    }
    print(total + kept[0]);
    return 0;
}
